ster eventually paved the way  for a deal  but it was an election that followed as  the commons became  stuck in a stalemate and refused to back  the prime minister's plans  vowing to get brexit done boris johnson  fought the campaign  promising an oven-ready deal the exit  poll  is predicting is indicating it's a  conservative  win the strategy worked a thumping  majority was gifted to the conservatives  brexit now certain but still far from  done  boris johnson promised an end to  squabbling about the eu  but in reality his divorce deal left  loose ends on one  key issue the new mechanism to keep the  irish border open  ceded powers to brussels and created a  new frontier in the irish sea something  the prime minister had previously said  he would never do  and that's why arguments over this  border flared again during this year's  talks  when boris johnson tabled laws that  would unpick  parts of the agreement he'd signed just  months before  they were eventually withdrawn but not  before this shock admission from a  cabinet minister  yes this does break international law in  a very specific and limited way  amid anger from the eu and with trust  running low  negotiations seem to be petering out i  concluded  that we should get ready for january the  first with arrangements  that are more like australia's based on  simple principles  of global free trade whether that was  genuine or just theatrics negotiations  did restart  mr belmir was a success in london but  with little progress to report  it was left to the politicians to  intervene  a dinner between boris johnson and the  eu commission president  was arranged at first the gaps between  the two sides seemed too wide  but then at the last minute a  breakthrough and a deal  so is this the end of the brexit story  we haven't really changed up  trade policy relative to third countries  if brexit means we can have our own  trade policy we're going to have to go  back and negotiate changes with these  other countries  so no no it's gonna run and run more  than 50 months since the country voted  to leave  we now have some clarity on the  relationship we'll have with our closest  neighbour  the true legacy of brexit though will be  how the uk  uses its newfound freedoms rob powell's  sky news  in westminster
 

